WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.037 --> 00:00:02.620
ROBERT KUBIS: In this episode
of "Things-I-Learned About Cloud

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:04.411
Spanner," we look at
write-batching to help

00:00:04.411 --> 00:00:07.380
a database specialist
accelerate their data load.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.850
Will we find the
right strategies

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:10.230
for higher throughputs?

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:11.651
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:11.651 --> 00:00:14.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.460
In the previous episode,
I helped my friend Anna,

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.290
and her company,
Pile Of Gadgets,

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.430
an online shop for gadget fans.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:23.310
We were able to solve
a hotspotting issue

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.740
during the data load that
kept the throughput low

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.680
and the Cloud Spanner
instance under-utilized.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.260
By choosing new,
well-distributed primary keys

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.360
for the tables,
we saw an increase

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.770
in throughput and utilization.

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:38.430
Now we are going to see if
we can improve the data load

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.920
performance even further.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.076
The data load app, which reads
the records from csv files

00:00:44.076 --> 00:00:45.450
and writes them
to Cloud Spanner,

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:48.270
was writing only one
row per transaction.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:51.270
Since our goal is to load
data as fast as possible,

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:53.010
loading with a single
row per transaction

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.690
doesn't sound like
the optimal solution.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:57.480
It seemed obvious
to batch as many

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.420
writes as possible together to
see a considerable throughput

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.990
boost, while staying below
transaction limits, of course.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.720
The records we were
loading are fairly small,

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.050
so we increased the batch size
to 1,000 rows per transaction,

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.150
and expected much
higher throughputs

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:15.070
due to the reduced client-server
communications and transaction

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:16.110
overhead.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.390
Surprisingly, we
only saw a bit more

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.160
than doubling of our
write throughput--

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.370
50,000 rows instead of
the previous 20,000 rows

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:24.090
per second.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:25.470
Don't get me wrong--

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.570
more than doubling the
throughput is great.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:31.530
But we wanted to see if we
can improve this even further.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.990
As it turns out, we were
still missing a step

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.420
to get the real
benefits of batching.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.220
The rows that were
batched together

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.200
have well-distributed
primary keys,

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.390
the ones we introduced
in the previous episode

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.090
to fix the hotspotting
issue during data load.

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.915
That means writing
1,000 of those rows

00:01:47.915 --> 00:01:51.240
with well-distributed primary
keys in a single transaction

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.250
ends up involving many,
many table splits.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:57.570
As you know, a table is
split based on size and load

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:00.570
patterns in ranges of rows
ordered alphabetically

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.130
by primary key.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.350
The responsibility
for the split is then

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:06.270
distributed among the
available compute nodes,

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:09.960
where each split is controlled
by exactly one node.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.120
Now, when we write to multiple
splits in a single transaction,

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.000
Cloud Spanner
needs to coordinate

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.450
between all the nodes that are
responsible for these splits.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.690
For that, it needs to obtain
locks and serialize the access

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:25.470
for all outstanding transactions
writing to any of these splits,

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.240
using the two-phase
commit protocol.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.000
This means the
benefits of having

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.430
multiple rows in one
transaction are reduced

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.140
by coordination
overhead incurred

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.390
by spanning multiple splits.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.850
Armed with this knowledge of
the inner workings of Cloud

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.880
Spanner, we needed a way to
reduce the number of splits

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:45.180
accessed by each transaction
to gain the maximum benefit

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:46.650
of batching.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:48.630
We went back to
our loader program

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:52.020
and modified it to load a large
number of rows into cache.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:55.230
We then sorted these rows
alphabetically by primary key--

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:57.330
the same way the table
splits are ordered--

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.060
and partitioned them by
our chosen batch size.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.950
From there, we created
one transaction

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.230
for each of the partitions.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:06.590
Some might ask here,
didn't we just introduce

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:08.670
well-distributed
primary keys to avoid

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.750
hotspotting in the
previous episode,

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:12.420
and now we are sorting?

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:13.200
Right.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.570
The trick here is that
the ordered partitions

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:18.780
are distinct in the primary
key ranges they write to.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:22.230
Spanning fewer table splits
ensues, causing less contention

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:23.610
during writes.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.710
As you can see in
the graph, this

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.960
improved our load
performance significantly,

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:32.230
to about 120,000 rows per
second, a six-fold increase

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:34.320
to where we started from.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.420
Now, to find the optimal
load performance,

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:39.240
we have to keep a couple
of limitations in mind.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.910
Besides optimal size
ranges for transactions,

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:45.330
between 1 to 10 megabytes,
there is a strict limitation

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.210
of 20,000 mutation
per transaction.

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:49.410
Mutation?

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:50.370
What is that?

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:53.020
A mutation has a
special meaning here,

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:56.810
where any modification of a
single cell in a table or index

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:00.090
accounts for one
mutation, except if you

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:02.010
delete a range of rows.

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:05.250
So for example, if you have
a table with five columns,

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:08.110
and an index defined on
three of those columns,

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:11.490
including the primary key
columns, each instance of a row

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:14.440
accounts for eight mutations.

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:16.529
Now, if you don't want
to write all this logic

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:19.380
into your own data loader,
you can use the Cloud Dataflow

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.209
connector for Cloud Spanner.

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:24.330
The connector does the
sorting, partitioning,

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.540
and scaling out writes
automatically for you.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:30.210
Check out the sample source code
we linked in the description

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.460
to learn more about it.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:35.010
After all these data
load optimizations,

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:36.580
my friend's company,
Pile of Gadgets,

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:38.430
was now happy with
the load performance,

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:40.680
and started to look
into how to optimize

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.440
the apps for scalability
and performance.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.840
Unfortunately, we are out
of time in this episode.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:48.835
So stay tuned for
our next one, where

00:04:48.835 --> 00:04:52.530
we show how to design our
transactions for scalability

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:53.840
and performance.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:57.190
[MUSIC PLAYING]

